\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Processing tomatoes are a high-volume crop in the Central Valley, and water and heat stress increasingly constrain yields.
Agrivoltaic designs place photovoltaic panels above the crop canopy, trading a share of the light for electricity and shade.
Growers have asked whether the trade leaves the crop viable.
\section{Methods}
The trial ran at a commercial farm in Yolo County, California, from 2023 to 2025.
Panel rows ran east-west at a fixed tilt of 32 degrees, with open control plots alongside.
Irrigation, fertility, and harvest timing followed the farm's normal practice.
Marketable yield was graded at the packing shed.
